# /rtc_cm/clear_estop — clear the CM GLOBAL E-STOP latch (issue #288).
#
# Until this service existed the global latch could be raised (device timeout,
# consecutive overrun, unholdable output) but never lowered from outside: the
# only production caller of ClearGlobalEstop() was on_deactivate, so a process
# restart was the only exit. #260 closed exactly this shape for the
# controller-local fault latch (/rtc_cm/reset_fault); this is its counterpart
# for the global one.
#
# Deliberately SEPARATE from /rtc_cm/reset_fault (E-8): clearing the global
# E-STOP does NOT clear a controller-local fault, and clearing a
# controller-local fault does NOT clear the global E-STOP. Both may be latched
# at once and each needs its own clear. `message` says when the other one is
# still up, so a reply is never read as "the arm is free to move" when it is
# not.
#
# Authority: the caller must echo the CURRENT latched reason. That is the
# operator confirmation step, and the global analogue of reset_fault requiring
# the active controller's name — there is no wildcard, so a script that never
# observed why the arm stopped cannot clear it. The reason is the string
# reported on /system/estop_status's log line and by the refusal message below.

# The currently latched E-STOP reason, echoed back verbatim. REQUIRED.
# Obtain it from the refusal message of a call with an empty ack, or from the
# "GLOBAL E-STOP triggered: <reason>" log line.
string reason_ack

---

# True iff the latch is confirmed down — it was lowered AND enough RT ticks
# completed for the cause detectors to have had a turn AND it was still down
# afterwards. Also true for the no-op case (nothing was latched).
#
# False means the arm is still held, and `message` distinguishes:
#   - reason_ack was empty or did not match the latched reason (nothing was
#     touched);
#   - something asked for an E-STOP while the clear was propagating to the
#     controllers, so the clear was abandoned and the latch LEFT UP. The
#     unguarded trigger paths (consecutive overrun, sim-sync abort) can land
#     here; the watchdog and output-validation paths test the latch first and
#     cannot;
#   - the cause is still present: a detector re-latched within the observation
#     window. The window spans at least one device-watchdog check, which is
#     slower than the control period — reset_fault's two-tick proof would not
#     have covered it;
#   - the RT loop completed no tick within the deadline, so nothing
#     re-evaluated the cause. NOTE the asymmetry with reset_fault here: the
#     latch IS down in this case, it is only unverified. `message` says so
#     explicitly.
bool ok

# Human-readable outcome, including the reason that was latched and whether a
# controller-local fault is still latched AT REPLY TIME.
string message
